      "text": "To find the largest divisor of 342 that is also a factor of 285, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Find the prime factorization of 342:**\n   \\[\n   342 \\div 2 = 171 \\quad \\text{(342 is even)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   171 \\div 3 = 57 \\quad \\text{(sum of digits of 171 is 9, so 171 is divisible by 3)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   57 \\div 3 = 19\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   19 \\div 19 = 1 \\quad \\text{(19 is a prime number)}\n   \\]\n   So, the prime factorization of 342 is:\n   \\[\n   342 = 2 \\times 3^2 \\times 19\n   \\]\n\n2. **Find the prime factorization of 285:**\n   \\[\n   285 \\div 3 = 95 \\quad \\text{(sum of digits of 285 is 15, so 285 is divisible by 3)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   95 \\div 5 = 19\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   19 \\div 19 = 1 \\quad \\text{(19 is a prime number)}\n   \\]\n   So, the prime factorization of 285 is:\n   \\[\n   285 = 3 \\times 5 \\times 19\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the common divisors of 342 and 285:**\n   The common divisors of 342 and 285 are the common prime factors, each raised to the lowest power that appears in both factorizations. The prime factors are 3 and 19.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Common divisors} = 3^1 \\times 19^1 = 57\n   \\]\n\n4. **Verify that 57 is a divisor of both 342 and 285:**\n   - For 342: \n     \\[\n     342 \\div 57 = 6 \\quad \\text{(an integer, so 57 is a divisor of 342)}\n     \\]\n   - For 285:\n     \\[\n     285 \\div 57 = 5 \\quad \\text{(an integer, so 57 is a divisor of 285)}\n     \\]\n\nSince 57 is a common divisor of both 342 and 285, and it is the largest such divisor, the largest divisor of 342 that is also a factor of 285 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{57}\n\\]",
